<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anthony St Leger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1496–<a href="/years/1559">1559</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sir Anthony St Leger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Anthony St Leger was an English administrator who served three times as lord deputy of Ireland between 1540 and 1556. He secured the Crown of Ireland Act of 1541, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> king of Ireland, and pursued the policy of surrender and regrant, persuading Gaelic and Gaelicised lords such as Conn O'Neill and Murrough O'Brien to accept English titles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anthony-st-leger.jpg" alt="Anthony St Leger" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rs-nourse · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Sir_Anthony_St._Leger,_KG.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
